{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"24a1b0c5fe5a412dcb04c94b4da033ffe978a9d21ea1aff1703369bff76b3338","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_2dd7ef09ed1642b4a4ca67cc1fe72f0987d953bf339f274480e75f6f6d9e4e0b","overall_assessment":"The paper presents a systematic exploration of DNN architectures for tabular data, combining a novel architecture generation method with a meta-learning approach for early performance prediction. The originality lies in the specific combination of topological and training-dynamic features for ranking, as well as the empirical finding regarding the effectiveness of parallel layers in this domain.","paper_id":"Byx5BTilg","paper_identity_sha256":"be48678dd0e9a8b769e83a97c71e50705c514b06f8fae468ddde88d3bc0938f0","prompt_identity_sha256":"75e26c9992427677a663d9b14c19dfc528bc3ef6a5ee7ce1bec20c899baf02db","raw_response_sha256":"6b5a829c1f37be929a5626275b8fce7c5d006acad05d7c88c98e6a3ab581aeac","request_identity_sha256":"19f2c070cb1236454b2b8edc3c1c1024201299eebe3eca41c183fd1a232c679d","reviewer_identity_sha256":"32b29c26fbf1508e066dfb25b1a9a3fe0654c5869a7da3020e1e8e61940befe3","role":"ORIG","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"ce67f84015c5cd305c8c34fd2d1b27418130501e0036e60980f57f7b4c67db21","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-010","SRC-011"],"review_text":"The paper frames its contribution around four specific research questions regarding the transferability of DNN architectures across domains, the effectiveness of non-deep (parallel) architectures, and the feasibility of early-stage performance prediction. While the individual questions are grounded in existing challenges of hyperparameter tuning and architecture search, the specific focus on tabular data and the systematic comparison of parallel versus serial topologies in this context provides a clear and motivated problem definition.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-PROB"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-013","SRC-014","SRC-017"],"review_text":"The authors introduce an algorithmic approach for the automatic generation of diverse DNN topologies, including parallel layers and connections between layers of varying depths. This generation method is presented as a tool to explore the architecture space systematically. The method is a procedural contribution that enables the subsequent analysis, though it relies on a fixed set of components and hyperparameters, which the authors acknowledge as a limitation.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-MTH"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-018","SRC-021","SRC-032"],"review_text":"The paper provides a novel analysis by applying meta-learning to rank architectures based on features extracted from the initial training steps, specifically modeling changes in weights, biases, and activation functions. The authors claim this is the first time these specific characteristics have been used in such a scheme. The analysis reveals that training-based features are more predictive than topological or dataset-based features alone, offering insight into the dynamics of DNN training that can be leveraged for efficiency.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-ANL"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-022","SRC-023"],"review_text":"The experimental setup involves a diverse set of tabular datasets with varying sizes and feature compositions, which is distinct from the image and speech datasets typically used in DNN research. The use of these specific datasets, combined with the protocol of training a large number of generated architectures on each, constitutes a meaningful experimental contribution that allows for the evaluation of architecture transferability and the meta-learning model's generalization.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-EXP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-018","SRC-019","SRC-020"],"review_text":"The paper combines existing techniques of meta-learning, topological feature extraction, and training-dynamic monitoring into a unified framework for architecture ranking. The rationale for this combination is well-articulated: the authors hypothesize that the interplay between data characteristics, network structure, and training dynamics determines performance. This combination yields a new capability to predict performance early in the training process, which is not achievable by any of the individual techniques alone.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-COM"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-025","SRC-026"],"review_text":"The paper presents a critical assessment of the common assumption that high-performing DNN architectures are transferable across different domains. The results demonstrate that architectures that perform well on one dataset do not necessarily perform well on others, challenging the idea of a universal 'best' architecture for general supervised learning. This negative result regarding transferability is a significant finding that informs future research on domain-specific architecture design.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-NEG"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"835d117af9994828196d82528ec88b50af8be6bed411f888b02c2d96ff19defb","paper_id":"Byx5BTilg","paper_ordinal":84,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"6b5a829c1f37be929a5626275b8fce7c5d006acad05d7c88c98e6a3ab581aeac","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"Byx5BTilg","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"1c54c5b48e990d4bb1c509fe00b66608fa3d5404dc8e554222ff6abbf2769f32","request_identity_sha256":"19f2c070cb1236454b2b8edc3c1c1024201299eebe3eca41c183fd1a232c679d","role":"ORIG","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v6","usage":{"completion_tokens":967,"prompt_tokens":16586,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":17553},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"19f2c070cb1236454b2b8edc3c1c1024201299eebe3eca41c183fd1a232c679d","role":"ORIG","role_ordinal":3,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}